<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.15.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02016</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.15.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a9c82f0e-6ad5-44bc-90e2-3c0a5bf6e8ce</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="c133d14f-dfe1-4d87-983d-a394d9d888d3" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para><emphasis>Select three</emphasis> quadratic expressions written in vertex form.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(y=(x-2)^2 + 1, y=x^2 - 4, y=(x-4)^2 + 6\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.14.2 – 7.14.3.</para><para>The correct answers are: \(y=(x-2)^2 + 1, y=x^2 - 4, y=(x-4)^2 + 6\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_7b0b1508-7cef-415d-80fd-59c5381859fa" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Examine the following two equations and use them to answer questions 2 – 5. One defines function \(m\) and the other defines function \(p\).</para><para>\(m(x)=x(x+6)\)</para>
        <para>\(p(x)=(x+3)^2−9\)</para>
      <para>How do you know that the expressions defining \(m\) and \(p\) are equivalent?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>Both expressions are equal to \(x^2+6x\).</para><para>Both expressions are equal to \(x^2-6x\).</para><para>Both expressions are equal to \(2x+6\).</para><para>Both expressions are equal to \(x^2-3\).</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! Both expressions are equal to \(x^2+6x\). \((x+3)^2−9=(x+3)(x+3)−9=x^2+3x+3x+9−9=x^2+6x\) and \(x(x+6)=x^2+6x\).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.14.2 and 7.14.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: Both expressions are equal to \(x^2+6x\). \((x+3)^2−9=(x+3)(x+3)−9=x^2+3x+3x+9−9=x^2+6x\) and \(x(x+6)=x^2+6x\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="db7fd02c-7b6a-46cf-b8e0-55761c276bb8" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Recall \(m(x)=x(x+6)\) and \(p(x)=(x+3)^2−9\). What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of \(m\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -3.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.4.</para><para>The correct answer is -3. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -3.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="c98bb928-4560-4c4b-bbf4-ea0149ca6437" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Recall \(m(x)=x(x+6)\) and \(p(x)=(x+3)^2−9\). What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of \(m\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -9.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.4.</para><para>The correct answer is -9. The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -9.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_32b1b9fd-9c8e-42a7-819e-b7a0dd0c09da" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Recall \(m(x)=x(x+6)\) and \(p(x)=(x+3)^2−9\). What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph of \(p\)?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\((0,0)\) and \((-6,0)\)</para><para>\((0,0)\) and \((6,0)\)</para><para>\((0,0)\) and \((0,-6)\)</para><para>\((0,0)\) and \((0,6)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! (0,0) and (-6,0) The graphs of \(m\) and \(p\) are the same. The zeros of \(m\) are 0 and -6, which give the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.3.</para><para>The correct answer is:(0,0) and (-6,0). The graphs of \(m\) and \(p\) are the same. The zeros of \(m\) are 0 and -6, which give the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_74d02381-b4db-4a6f-905a-364096b5a335" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>For questions 6 – 11, enter the coordinates of the vertex of the graph that represents the equation.</para><para>Find the coordinates of the vertex for the equation \(y=(x-3)^2 + 5\).</para>
      <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      
    </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is 3.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is 3. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is 3.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_8db0a037-729b-45cc-99dd-29ba0eb6651d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is 5.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is 5. The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is 5.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_531c5ab7-b573-41b0-a0e6-72810f62ea29" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the coordinates of the vertex for the equation \(y=(x+7)^2 + 3\).</para>
      <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -7.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is -7. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -7.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_835917d0-a687-4630-ab31-da7b539992df" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is 3.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is 3. The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is 3.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_50d774b8-4213-4075-b64d-5439c4ae7b4e" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the coordinates of the vertex for the equation \(y=(x-4)^2\).</para>
      <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is 4.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is 4. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is 4.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="b848525e-136a-4158-9b48-dea628bbb367" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is 0.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is 0. The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is 0.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_56fcc5c0-1b76-4db6-b2c1-e86e82d17fe6" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the coordinates of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2 - 1\).</para>
      <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is 0.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is 0. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is 0.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_077890de-2a93-4c41-86e7-4bb7e43bb860" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -1.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is -1. The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -1.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="faed0a60-b173-4c1a-8e71-93017a212011" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the coordinates of the vertex for the equation \(y=2(x+1)^2-5\).</para>
      <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -1.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is -1. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -1.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="dba4eaa5-0b15-41d6-9fd8-0177f93f2551" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -5.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is -5. The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -5.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_17608d6c-e24e-49a4-b113-55d85b08d20a" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the coordinates of the vertex for the equation \(y=-2(x+1)^2-5\).</para>
      <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -1.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is -1. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -1.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_547a2c12-3f38-42b2-ad64-7aef3013dbb9" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -5.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is -5. The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -5.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_153bf41c-176e-4750-8244-eb98aa10b803" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>For questions 12 – 14, answer the questions about the \(x\)- and \(y\)-coordinates of the vertex, and whether the function opens up or down.</para><para>For function \(f(x)=(x-4)^2-5\), find the coordinates of the vertex and determine if the function opens up or down.</para>
      <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      
    </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is 4.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is 4. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is 4.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_4d8843ca-418a-42ba-bd8c-07a677701d43" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -5.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is -5. The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -5.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="ddb461ca-54ff-4d3a-938d-267147fd2d73" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>For the function \(f(x)=(x-4)^2-5\), does the graph open up or down?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The graph opens upward. The squared term has a positive coefficient (1).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is Up. The graph opens upward. The squared term has a positive coefficient (1).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_63a5f778-bcb8-4fb8-9154-e4031dffe0b6" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>For function \(g(x)=-x^2+5\), find the coordinates of the vertex and determine if the function opens up or down.</para>
      <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is 0.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is 0. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is 0.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="fe6eeb1a-1ac8-45e3-ba14-ccddaacb489d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is 5.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is 5. The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is 5.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_5b4be5d0-00e7-4d0b-af7a-57111623a19f" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>For the function \(g(x)=-x^2+5\), does the graph open up or down?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The graph opens downward. The squared term has a negative coefficient (-1).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is Down. The graph opens downward. The squared term has a negative coefficient (-1).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="e6373ada-eda7-4bca-a713-f19d5ed95b7d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>For function \(h(x)=2(x+1)^2-4\), find the coordinates of the vertex and determine if the function opens up or down.</para>
      <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -1.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is -1. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -1.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_74f6c292-8f70-41d2-a76f-4210efb1c5a7" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -4.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is -4. The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -4.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_9476e3df-31f2-48b7-8072-1cc2d74d2729" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>For the function \(h(x)=2(x+1)^2-4\), does the graph open up or down?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The graph opens upward. The squared term has a positive coefficient (2).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.4.</para><para>The correct answer is Up. The graph opens upward. The squared term has a positive coefficient (2).</para></solution></exercise></section></section>
